The '''2026 Burawi snap leadership election''' was held on 19 April 2026 to elect the [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]]. The election was triggered by the successful parliamentary recall vote against incumbent Leader [[Hancil Thea]] on 20 March 2026, which required the dissolution of Parliament and the scheduling of snap elections within thirty days pursuant to Article 49 of the [[Constitution of Burawa]].

The election was the second leadership election conducted under {{w|ranked-choice voting}}, following the system's introduction in the [[2025 Burawi leadership election]]. [[Muna Xasan]] of the [[Party for Equitable Progress]] won the election with 58.0 per cent of the vote after ranked-choice redistribution, defeating Thea in the final round. Xasan's victory made her the first female [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] in Burawi history.

The election represented an unprecedented constitutional crisis in Burawi history, as a recalled Leader sought to regain executive authority through a fresh popular mandate. Xasan's campaign emphasised opposition to the Industrial Party agreement that had preserved Thea's administration during the recall process, as well as progressive reform and her potential to become Burawa's first female Leader.

== Background ==

{{main|2026 Burawi leadership recall vote}}

The 2026 snap election was triggered by the successful parliamentary recall of [[Hancil Thea]] from the office of Leader of the Burawi Republic. The recall motion, which required approval by seventy-five per cent of Parliament under Article 47 of the [[Constitution of Burawa]], was the first such successful recall in Burawi history.

=== First recall vote (13 March 2026) ===

The first parliamentary recall vote, held on 13 March 2026, received 754 votes in favour (73.5 per cent), falling 16 votes short of the 770 votes required for passage. The [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]] abstained from the vote, while the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] and [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Christian Socialist Union]] voted against.

=== Industrial Party agreement ===

{{main|Business Tax Reduction Act of 2026}}

On 19 March 2026, the Thea administration announced an agreement with the Industrial Party. Under the terms of the agreement, the government committed to reducing the corporate tax rate on private enterprises from 30 per cent to 25 per cent for a period of five years. In exchange, the Industrial Party agreed to oppose the second recall motion.

=== Second recall vote (20 March 2026) ===

The second parliamentary recall vote, held on 20 March 2026, received 812 votes in favour (79.1 per cent), exceeding the required threshold. The Industrial Party's 58 members voted against the motion, along with the National Party and Christian Socialist Union. Following the passage of the recall, Thea announced that snap elections would be scheduled for 19 April 2026 and declared his intention to stand for re-election.

=== Parliamentary breach ===

{{main|2026 Burawi leadership recall protests}}

Following the announcement of the recall result, supporters of Thea breached the perimeter of the parliamentary precinct in [[Kankadadka]], resulting in a violent confrontation with security forces. The use of tear gas and water cannons against demonstrators marked the most significant civil unrest in the capital since the [[1962 Crisis]].

== Candidates ==

=== National Party ===

{{main|National Party of Burawa}}

The [[National Party of Burawa]] confirmed on 21 March 2026 that [[Hancil Thea]] would serve as the party's candidate for the snap election. Thea, who was elected Leader in the 2025 election under the ranked-choice voting system, was recalled from office on 20 March 2026. His re-election bid represents an attempt to regain the executive authority removed through parliamentary process.

The National Party's platform emphasises rural investment, agricultural development, and opposition to metropolitan dominance. Thea's campaign is expected to frame the recall as an illegitimate attempt by urban parties to overturn the will of rural voters.

=== Socialist Party ===

{{main|Socialist Party of Burawa}}

The [[Socialist Party of Burawa]] announced on 22 March 2026 that [[Xasan Garaad]] would serve as the party's candidate for the snap election. Garaad, who placed first in first-preference voting in the 2025 election with 30 per cent of the vote, was defeated in the final round of ranked-choice redistribution by Thea.

The Socialist Party's platform emphasises public-sector wage reform, traditional socialist governance, and opposition to Thea's rural-focused policies. Garaad's campaign is expected to highlight the recall vote as evidence of Thea's unfitness for office and the need for metropolitan leadership.

=== Forward Party ===

{{main|Forward Party of Burawa}}

On 26 March 2026, [[Marik Sahamin]], the former Leader who served from 2015 until his electoral defeat in 2025, announced his candidacy for the snap election. The announcement triggered an immediate split within the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]]. Approximately 60 per cent of the party's parliamentary caucus defected to form the '''[[Forward Progressive Party]]''', led by [[Mahad Allaale]]. The Forward Progressive Party announced it would not field a candidate and instead endorsed [[Muna Xasan]] of the [[Party for Equitable Progress]], citing ideological alignment with her progressive platform and opposition to Sahamin's leadership.

Sahamin retained the support of approximately 40 per cent of the Forward Party's seats and continues to campaign under the Forward Party banner. His candidacy represents an attempt to regain the leadership after his 2025 defeat, though polling indicates he trails significantly behind Xasan, Thea, and Garaad.

