The [[Communist Party of Burawa|Communist Party]], [[Green Party of Burawa|Green Party]], and [[Northern Socialist Party]] have not announced whether they will nominate candidates for the leadership election or maintain their support for the recall coalition's candidate. The [[Party for Equitable Progress]] announced on 25 March 2026 that [[Muna Xasan]] would serve as its candidate for the snap election. The party, which had abstained from both recall votes, stated that its decision to nominate a candidate stemmed from opposition to the agreement between the Thea administration and the [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]] that had reduced corporate taxes from 30 per cent to 25 per cent. Party leaders characterised the agreement as a corrupt bargain that undermined the recall process and betrayed the progressive coalition's principles. If elected, Xasan would become the first female [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] in Burawi history.

=== Withdrawn candidates ===

On 3 April 2026, [[Faarax Mikael]] of the [[Reformed Christian Socialist Party (Burawa)|Reformed Christian Socialist Party]] announced his withdrawal from the race and endorsed [[Muna Xasan]]. Mikael, who had received 3 per cent of first-preference votes in the 2025 election, cited the need to consolidate the progressive vote and prevent a split that could benefit Thea's re-election bid.

On 7 April 2026, [[Bile Sago]] of the [[Sago Revolutionary Party]] announced his withdrawal from the race and endorsed [[Hancil Thea]] of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]]. Sago, who had received 1 per cent of first-preference votes in the 2025 election, stated that Thea's rural development policies aligned with Sago's regional interests and that the recall had been an undemocratic overturning of the 2025 mandate.

== Campaign issues ==

=== Constitutional legitimacy ===

The central issue of the campaign is the constitutional legitimacy of the recall process and Thea's bid for re-election. Thea's supporters argue that the recall was orchestrated by metropolitan parties seeking to overturn the legitimate result of the 2025 election, and that the snap election represents an opportunity for the electorate to confirm its mandate. Opponents argue that Thea's administration demonstrated unfitness for office through the military airport directive, the Fure commutation, and the Industrial Party agreement, and that the recall represented a legitimate exercise of parliamentary authority.

=== The Industrial Party agreement ===

{{main|Business Tax Reduction Act of 2026}}

The agreement between the Thea administration and the Industrial Party, which reduced corporate taxes from 30 per cent to 25 per cent in exchange for opposition to the second recall vote, has emerged as a significant campaign issue. Socialist and Forward Party officials have characterised the agreement as a "political bribe," while National Party officials have defended it as legitimate economic policy reform.

=== Regional divide ===

The campaign has highlighted the regional divide between rural agricultural communities, which form the National Party's electoral base, and metropolitan areas, which support the Socialist and Forward parties. Thea's supporters frame the recall as an attempt by urban elites to overturn the will of rural voters, while opposition parties argue that Thea's policies favour rural interests at the expense of metropolitan development.

== Debates ==

The first televised debate of the campaign was held on 8 April 2026, featuring all six candidates: [[Muna Xasan]], [[Hancil Thea]], [[Xasan Garaad]], [[Marik Sahamin]], [[Liban Qaasim]], and [[Giorgis Nuur]]. The debate, moderated by the [[Burawi Broadcasting Corporation]], focused on the constitutional legitimacy of the recall, economic policy, and regional development. Analysts widely assessed Xasan as the debate's winner, citing her articulate critique of the Industrial Party agreement and her appeal to both progressive and moderate voters.

A second debate was held on 12 April 2026, with all six candidates participating. The debate addressed healthcare reform, infrastructure investment, and foreign policy. Xasan's performance reinforced her frontrunner status, while Garaad and Thea engaged in heated exchanges over the recall's legitimacy.

